ITEM 13 -- the Local Group timing argument with no free mass
====================================================================================================================
  canonical  MOND two-body, M_b = 1.80e+11 Msun, no dark matter, no free parameter: at t = 13.8 Gyr the separation is 0.780 Mpc and the relative velocity is -223 km/s   (MEASURED: 0.78 Mpc, -110 km/s)
  alt        MOND two-body, M_b = 1.80e+11 Msun, no dark matter, no free parameter: at t = 13.8 Gyr the separation is 0.780 Mpc and the relative velocity is -241 km/s   (MEASURED: 0.78 Mpc, -110 km/s)
  [PASS] 13 AGAINST INTEREST -- the simple radial MOND timing argument OVER-predicts the approach: with the measured baryons and no dark matter it gives -223 (canonical) / -241 (alt) km/s at the observed 0.78 Mpc, against a measured -110.  A factor two too fast, both footings   (canonical -223 km/s, alt -241 vs measured -110; the Newtonian version needs 5e12 Msun, 25x the measured baryons, so neither is comfortable)
  the published MOND treatment (Zhao, Famaey, Luhausen & Kroupa 2013) resolves this the interesting way: MOND's stronger pull means
  the pair must already have had a CLOSE ENCOUNTER, 7-11 Gyr ago, and is now on its second approach -- which a purely radial
  single-passage integration like the one above cannot represent.  That flyby is a distinctive, testable prediction (it would have
  stripped and reshaped both discs, and is the proposed origin of the Local Group's satellite planes); this item should be re-run
  with a two-passage orbit before being read either way.  Recorded as OVER-PREDICTED BY THE SIMPLE MODEL, not as a kill.
  caveat both ways: a radial two-body orbit ignores the transverse velocity (small, ~20 km/s from HST/Gaia) and the Local Group's
  own mass beyond the two galaxies; the Newtonian version of this argument needs 5e12 Msun, which is 25x the measured baryons.

